#### Cross-Section estimation

In order to make meaningful comparisons to theory and other experiments it is important to estimate a production cross-section of the observed'signal'.

First, we need to assume a branching ratio of the observed channel, \(\theta^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{K^{0}_{s}}+p\), to be 25%, due to an equal 50% splitting into the two decay modes, and an additional 50% due to the possible \(K^{0}_{L}\) final state, which is not measured in STAR.

Second, we can now apply the acceptance and efficiency corrections computed with the MC simulation using our reconstruction cuts. We need to estimate a correction factor for the phase-space region in \(p_{T}\) and rapidity in which we measure our signal. These values are 1.2% and 0.2% for \(p+p\) and \(d+Au\) respectively with a statistical and systematical error of about 20%.

