#### Conclusion

We have analyzed data from \(p+p\) and \(d+Au\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=\)200 GeV in the STAR experiment. We have selected our cuts in favor of a proposed \(\theta^{+}\) production mechanism via an N*(2400) resonance. We see a peak structure in the K\({}_{\rm s}^{0}\) +p invariant mass around 1550 MeV with a width of 6 MeV and 4 Mev in \(d+Au\) collision respectively \(p+p\) collisions. The statistical significance, \(S/\sqrt{B}\), is \(\approx 3.0\) for \(p+p\) and \(\approx 5.5\) for \(d+Au\) collisions.

Using Monte-Carlo simulation we have estimated the reconstruction efficiency and obtained relative \(\theta^{+}\) cross-sections with respect to \(\Lambda^{*}(1520)\) and \(\Lambda\) measured by STAR. The \(\theta^{+}/\Lambda^{*}(1520)\) results are in agreement with being below the Upper Limit (UL) published by HERA-B experiment, while the \(\theta^{+}/\Lambda\) results are below the upper limit as shown in table 6.8.

